Each applicant shall:
(1) Be at least 21 years old.
(2) Be a citizen of the United States, an alien admitted for permanent residence, or has otherwise been granted employment authorization by the United States Immigration and Naturalization Service.
(3) Have not been convicted of any felony crime within the ten-year period prior to the date of application.
(4) Exhibit a proficiency in the English language so as to be able to comprehend traffic signs and obey lawful orders of the police.
(5) The application shall contain the following information:
a. Name;
b. Age;
c. Race;
d. Sex;
e. Date of birth;
f. Social Security number;
g. Place of birth;
h. Nationality;
i. Alien identification number if applicant is not a United States citizen;
j. Residence address and telephone number;
k. Business address and telephone number;
l. Experience or training of applicant in transporting passengers for hire;
m. A complete disclosure of all arrests for criminal and traffic violations; and
n. Such other relevant information as shall be required by the city.
(6) Each application shall be accompanied by:
a. A valid Georgia driver's license;
b. One fingerprint card prepared by a member of a Georgia law enforcement agency;
c. A driver's history from the Georgia Department of Public Safety containing the applicant's driving record for the previous seven years;
d. Criminal history consent form;
e. Certification that he/she has read and understands this article; and
f. Two current two-inch by two-inch full-faced photographs of the applicant.
(Ord. No. 2012-05, 4-9-2012)

1587:
The City of Cleveland Zoning Ordinance is designed to implement the provisions of the comprehensive plan for the development and use of land. The purpose of these regulations shall be to:
(1) Promote the health, safety, morals, order, prosperity, and general welfare of the city;
(2) Promote desirable living conditions and the sustained stability of neighborhoods;
(3) Promote the proper location, height, bulk, number of stories, and size of buildings and other structures;
(4) Promote the proper sizes of yards, courts, and other open spaces;
(5) Protect property against blight and depreciation;
(6) Lessen congestion on streets;
(7) Secure safety from fire, panic, and other dangers;
(8) Provide adequate light and air and preventing the overcrowding of land;
(9) Avoid undue concentration of population;
(10) Facilitate the adequate provision of transportation, water, sewerage, schools, parks, and other public requirements;
(11) Promote a balance of residential, commercial, and industrial uses throughout the community;
(12) Discourage urban sprawl;
(13) Encourage the use of ecodevelopment, green building, sustainable development, and LEED (Leadership in Energy and Environmental Design) practices, as defined herein; and
(14) Promote the most appropriate use of land, buildings, and structures throughout the city in accordance with the comprehensive plan.
(Ord. No. 2024-26, 10-15-2024)

